<h1 id="s1">1. Contribution Statement</h1>
<p>The project ownership record assigns Student 2 to <b>Hospital Requests and Triage</b> and the <b>Coordinator Agent</b> [1]. My personal work in this assigned area was: [describe the implementation, design, testing, review and integration work you actually completed]. The system feature provides a traceable path for a hospital to submit a blood request and for authorized staff to review its urgency and status. The Coordinator Agent prepares a bounded plan that the other workflow stages can carry out as proposals. Human administrators remain responsible for approval and dispatch decisions.</p>
<div class="evidence"><b>Insert Evidence 1 — Ownership:</b> attach the project board, group allocation or contribution record that connects your own name/ID to this component and agent. Add the source and date.</div>

<h1 id="s2">2. Owned Component</h1>
<h2>2.1 Hospital requests</h2><p>A hospital requester must be linked to a hospital profile. The hospital registration record includes an official registration number, address, optional coordinates and verification status. Only a verified hospital can submit or update requests. A request records blood type, quantity, urgency, notes, a required-by time, current status, requester and hospital.</p>
<h2>2.2 Triage and lifecycle</h2><p>The request moves through states such as Submitted, UnderReview, PendingApproval, Approved, Dispatched, Fulfilled, Escalated and ClosedUnfulfilled. Only defined transitions are accepted. Before approval, a hospital may correct, escalate or withdraw its request. After approval, the API protects committed operations; dispatch also removes units from inventory in the same relational transaction. The request history records the old and new state, actor, reason and time.</p>
<h2>2.3 Why the feature matters</h2><p>Blood requests are time-sensitive and need enough structure for a blood bank to assess what is required, when it is needed and how the request is progressing. A shared status trail can reduce uncertainty between the requesting hospital and the blood bank. Verification, bounded inputs and explicit human approval are important because an incorrect request or unauthorized action could lead to unsafe resource handling. LifeLink is a coordination prototype, not a clinical triage authority or a replacement for established transfusion services.</p>
<p>ADR-01 assigns this domain and agent pairing to Student 2. It also says cross-cutting work must identify its implementer and reviewer and that student numbers need to be mapped to actual identities by the group [1].</p>

<h1 id="s3">3. Technical Implementation</h1>
<h2>3.1 Request intake and urgency</h2><p>The API validates blood type, urgency, quantity, notes and required-by time. The service converts the requested time to UTC and trims or clears blank notes. The required time must be in the future and within 30 days; quantity must be 1–100. Urgency is calculated from time remaining and requested quantity, then the higher of the user-provided and calculated levels is retained. In the implementation, a request due within two hours or for at least six units is Critical; one due within twelve hours or for at least three units is Urgent; otherwise it is Routine. This is an operational rule in the project, not a clinical standard.</p>
<h2>3.2 Workflow hand-off</h2><p>After a valid request is stored, the request service invokes an <code>IRequestWorkflowTrigger</code> with a reason such as request-created, request-updated or request-escalated. The workflow service creates a numbered execution, sends a structured request to the agent service, and persists plan, steps, outcome and approvals. Edits made before approval supersede an earlier proposal and request a new analysis. Withdrawing a request retires a pending proposal and releases any held reservation in the covered lifecycle paths.</p>
<h2>3.3 Failure and action controls</h2><p>The agent proposes a plan but cannot itself approve or dispatch. The workflow service validates agent output again, checks reservation quantity against remaining demand, checks recipients against active eligible donors of the requested type, and records an approval decision. The request API applies allowed-transition rules. Dispatch is protected by a database transaction and request-row lock in relational operation so stock removal and status change commit together. A failed action is surfaced as an error or escalation rather than being reported as completed.</p>

<h1 id="s4">4. Backend/API Evidence</h1><p>Primary source files are <code>backend/LifeLink.Api/Controllers/RequestsController.cs</code>, <code>backend/LifeLink.Application/Requests/RequestContracts.cs</code>, <code>backend/LifeLink.Infrastructure/Requests/RequestService.cs</code>, <code>backend/LifeLink.Infrastructure/Workflows/WorkflowService.cs</code> and <code>backend/LifeLink.Api/Controllers/AgentWorkflowsController.cs</code>.</p>
<table><tr><th>Route / behavior</th><th>Evidence and authorization</th></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/requests</code></td><td>HospitalRequester-only. Creates a request for the signed-in user’s linked, verified hospital.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /api/requests</code> and <code>GET /{id}</code></td><td>Admins can filter across hospitals; hospital staff are limited to their own hospital. Request-level access is checked before returning details/history.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PUT /{id}</code>, <code>DELETE /{id}</code>, <code>POST /{id}/escalate</code></td><td>Hospital requester actions are limited to its own request and pre-approval states; escalation reason is bounded.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PUT /{id}/status</code></td><td>Admin-only. Status transitions are checked; dispatch couples inventory change and request transition.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /{id}/history</code>, <code>GET /reports/summary</code></td><td>History is access-checked. Summary is scoped to the signed-in hospital for non-admin users.</td></tr></table>
<p>The API returns conflict responses for invalid transitions, closed requests, pending workflows and similar state conflicts. Input data annotations complement the deeper service rules. Pagination is clamped to a maximum page size of 100.</p>
<div class="evidence"><b>Insert Evidence 2 — Backend/API:</b> capture the request controller or service validation, plus a redacted request/response from Swagger or an API client. Show route and role context, not access tokens or personal data.</div>

<h1 id="s5">5. Database Evidence</h1><p>Core entities are in <code>backend/LifeLink.Domain/Entities/DonorHospitalEntities.cs</code> and <code>backend/LifeLink.Domain/Entities/WorkflowEntities.cs</code>. Relevant PostgreSQL tables configured in <code>LifeLinkDbContext.cs</code> include <code>hospitals</code>, <code>hospital_staff</code>, <code>blood_requests</code>, <code>request_status_history</code>, <code>agent_workflow_executions</code>, <code>agent_steps</code> and <code>agent_approvals</code>.</p>
<p>A hospital may have multiple staff links; each staff user is uniquely linked to one hospital profile in the current mapping. A blood request references its hospital and requesting user. Request history references the request and the user who made the change. Workflow executions reference a request and have a unique attempt number per request; their correlation ID is unique. Agent steps use unique workflow-and-sequence pairs, while approval records use unique workflow-and-version pairs. Foreign keys use restrictive delete behavior to preserve referenced operational records. Request quantity has a positive-value check constraint, and the status/urgency pair and hospital/creation-time are indexed for common triage queries.</p>
<p>These are schema facts from EF Core configuration. A production database still needs migration and constraint verification, backup/retention design and operational monitoring. Avoid placing unnecessary personal or clinical details in free-text notes.</p>
<div class="evidence"><b>Insert Evidence 3 — Database/EER:</b> add the relevant EER subset showing Hospital, HospitalStaff, BloodRequest, RequestStatusHistory, AgentWorkflowExecution, AgentStep and AgentApproval. Mark PKs, FKs and cardinalities. A screenshot of the EF mapping or PostgreSQL schema can support the diagram.</div>

<h1 id="s6">6. React Evidence</h1><p><code>web/src/pages/RequestsPage.tsx</code> provides the blood-bank request triage queue. It supports filtering by request status, shows blood type, quantity, hospital, urgency, notes, required-by time and current state, and offers state transitions defined for each current state. When a request is dispatched, it displays a message that stock was removed and refreshes stock-related queries. Related workflow review is represented in <code>web/src/pages/WorkflowsPage.tsx</code>.</p>
