<h1 id="s8">8. Agentic AI Contribution</h1><h2>8.1 Coordinator responsibility</h2><p>The Coordinator Agent lives in <code>agentic-ai/coordinator_agent/agent.py</code>. It calls <code>create_plan</code> with the request ID, blood type, quantity and urgency. It records a structured first step named CoordinatorAgent and places the plan into the shared graph state. It is the first node in the fixed LangGraph sequence: Coordinator, Domain Analysis, Dispatch, Validation [2].</p>
<h2>8.2 Bounded planning and fallback</h2><p><code>agentic-ai/lifelink_agents/model.py</code> asks an optional local Ollama model for JSON with an objective and an exact allow-list of three steps: analyse-domain, draft-dispatch and validate-safety. It bounds the objective length, timeout (up to ten seconds) and attempt count (up to three). A malformed response, timeout, unavailable provider or non-allow-listed step leads to a deterministic fallback plan. The model is identified in the plan so the execution is explainable at a basic operational level. The coordinator does not receive tools that approve requests, reserve stock or contact donors.</p>
<h2>8.3 Human authority and safeguards</h2><p>The Coordinator Agent organizes work; it does not decide clinical priority or authorize real-world action. Deterministic code calculates request urgency and later validates agent proposals. Human administrators approve/reject proposals through workflow endpoints. The group ADR explicitly keeps business rules and approval gates outside model authority [2].</p>
<p><b>My agent work:</b> [describe the plan schema, prompt/fallback, graph integration, agent tests or review that you personally completed. Include a concrete input and resulting plan that you can explain.]</p>
